# TLS termination for both sites.
#
# Caddy rather than certbot+cron: it obtains and renews certificates on its own,
# for every hostname named below, with no scheduled job to notice has stopped
# running. An expired certificate is a whole-store outage, and the usual cause
# is a renewal timer nobody was watching.
#
# It terminates TLS and forwards to the `web` container, which is the nginx that
# already knows how to split the two sites by hostname and proxy /api. Caddy
# deliberately does not learn any of that — it passes Host through, and nginx's
# existing server_name matching does the routing exactly as it does over plain
# HTTP locally. One place decides how requests are routed.
{
	# Let's Encrypt sends expiry and problem notices here. Set ACME_EMAIL in the
	# root .env; without it Caddy registers anonymously and you get no warning
	# when issuance starts failing.
	email {$ACME_EMAIL}
}

# Shared by both sites. Anything that must hold for the console as well as the
# shop goes here, so the two cannot drift apart.
(edge) {
	# HSTS belongs at the TLS edge, not in nginx. nginx serves plain HTTP inside
	# the compose network and is also what a local `docker compose up` uses with
	# no certificates at all — sending HSTS from there would teach a browser to
	# force HTTPS against a development stack that has none, and the only cure is
	# clearing the browser's HSTS store by hand.
	#
	# No `preload`. Preloading is a one-way door: it is baked into browser
	# binaries and removal takes months, so it must be a deliberate decision made
	# after HTTPS has been stable for a while, not a default inherited from a
	# config file. Add it yourself once you are sure.
	#
	# `includeSubDomains` covers app.admin.<domain>, which is served here over
	# HTTPS too. Drop it if you host any *other* subdomain over plain HTTP — it
	# would make that host unreachable.
	header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"

	# nginx already compresses what it serves and proxies; this covers Caddy
	# answering anything itself and costs nothing when the upstream has done it.
	encode zstd gzip

	# Caddy sets X-Forwarded-For/-Proto/-Host on its own; setting them again here
	# is redundant and `caddy validate` says so. What matters is downstream:
	# nginx *appends* to X-Forwarded-For, so the backend sees "<client>, <caddy>"
	# and must be told two proxies sit in front of it rather than one — see
	# TRUST_PROXY in docker-compose.prod.yml. Left at one, every visitor shares a
	# rate-limit bucket.
	reverse_proxy web:80
}

# ---- storefront ----
{$SITE_DOMAIN} {
	import edge
}

# www -> apex, so the site has one canonical origin. The sitemap, the canonical
# link tags and the og: tags all name VITE_SITE_URL; serving the same pages on a
# second hostname splits them in search results.
www.{$SITE_DOMAIN} {
	redir https://{$SITE_DOMAIN}{uri} permanent
}

# ---- admin console ----
# Matches the hostname nginx's second server block expects. This must also be a
# DNS record pointing here, and must be in Firebase's authorized domains, or
# Google sign-in on the console fails with auth/unauthorized-domain.
app.admin.{$SITE_DOMAIN} {
	import edge
}
